  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The recommended step to enhance self-healing in this setup is to <b>configure the RDS database with Multi-AZ deployments to enable automatic failover</b>. Multi-AZ provides automatic failover to a standby in case of a primary instance failure, ensuring higher availability and minimizing downtime, which are key aspects of a self-healing architecture.<br><br>The option <i>Set up additional read replicas for the AWS RDS database</i> improves read scalability but does not contribute to automatic failover or recovery in the event of a primary database failure. <i>Launch another Auto Scaling Group in a different region to improve fault tolerance</i> is not feasible because Auto Scaling Groups and ELBs are regional constructs, and cross-region failover for such components adds significant complexity not directly tied to self-healing. <i>Deploy a new ELB in a separate region for added redundancy</i> also does not enable self-healing, as ELBs do not span multiple regions and would further complicate architecture management.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> For database high availability in AWS, the Multi-AZ feature is recommended as it allows AWS RDS to automatically detect and recover from failures, reducing manual intervention and downtime.</p>",
  "query": "<p>An AWS deployment includes EC2 instances that are managed by an Auto Scaling Group and are serving requests behind an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB). These instances utilize a MySQL RDS database. What is another measure you could implement to make the architecture more self-healing and resilient to failure?</p>",
